1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:10,387
On the nineteenth of August, 2003, a truck packed with explosives pulled up beside the United Nations headquarters in Baghdad.

2
00:00:11,487 --> 00:00:19,465
The blast tore through the building, just below the office of the UN's top envoy in Iraq, Sérgio Vieira de Mello.

3
00:00:20,565 --> 00:00:29,312
Twenty-two people were killed, de Mello among them. Within weeks, the UN began pulling most of its foreign staff out of the country.

4
00:00:30,412 --> 00:00:41,527
The attack was the work of a Jordanian militant on whom the United States would soon put a twenty-five-million-dollar reward, the same as for Osama bin Laden.

5
00:00:42,627 --> 00:00:56,557
His aim was not only to drive out the Americans. It was to set Iraq's Sunni and Shia Muslims against each other. This is the story of Abu Musab al-Zarqawi, whose movement became the Islamic State.

6
00:00:58,757 --> 00:01:12,752
He was born Ahmad Fadil al-Khalayleh in 1966, in Zarqa, a poor industrial city near Amman, the capital of Jordan. He later took his fighting name from his home town.

7
00:01:13,852 --> 00:01:22,577
He left school at seventeen and drifted into petty crime and heavy drinking. He was arrested more than once, and spent time in jail.

8
00:01:23,677 --> 00:01:34,216
Worried, his mother sent him to religious classes at a local mosque. He took up a hard-line form of Islam with the same intensity he had brought to the streets.

9
00:01:35,316 --> 00:01:47,519
In 1989 he travelled to Afghanistan to fight the Soviet occupation, but arrived as the Soviet troops were leaving. He stayed on among the foreign jihadists.

10
00:01:48,619 --> 00:01:59,755
There he came to know Abu Muhammad al-Maqdisi, a Jordanian cleric whose writings condemned Arab governments as un-Islamic. Maqdisi became his mentor.

11
00:02:01,955 --> 00:02:12,643
Back in Jordan, the two men were arrested in 1994 for plotting attacks and possessing weapons. Zarqawi was sentenced to fifteen years.

12
00:02:13,743 --> 00:02:24,559
In prison he hardened. Fellow inmates and officials later described a man who memorised the Quran, lifted weights, and bullied the other prisoners into line.

13
00:02:25,659 --> 00:02:38,971
In 1999, Jordan's new king, Abdullah the Second, declared a general amnesty to mark his accession. Zarqawi walked free after about five years, and soon left the country.

14
00:02:40,071 --> 00:02:51,527
He returned to Afghanistan and ran a training camp near Herat, with help from al-Qaeda. But he did not swear loyalty to Osama bin Laden. He wanted his own movement.

15
00:02:53,727 --> 00:03:02,836
After the attacks of September the eleventh, the United States invaded Afghanistan. Zarqawi fled through Iran and reached Iraq.

16
00:03:03,936 --> 00:03:14,368
For a time he was linked to Ansar al-Islam, a militant group based in the Kurdish mountains of northern Iraq, beyond the control of Saddam's government.

17
00:03:15,468 --> 00:03:29,633
In October 2002, an American diplomat, Laurence Foley, was shot dead outside his home in Amman. A Jordanian court later sentenced Zarqawi to death in his absence for organising it.

18
00:03:30,733 --> 00:03:45,645
In February 2003, the US Secretary of State, Colin Powell, told the UN Security Council that Zarqawi was a link between Saddam Hussein's regime and al-Qaeda. It was part of the case for war.

19
00:03:46,745 --> 00:03:57,668
A US Senate investigation later found that Saddam's government had no such relationship with him. But the speech had made an obscure militant famous.

20
00:03:58,768 --> 00:04:10,053
The invasion came in March 2003, and Saddam's government fell within three weeks. Into the chaos that followed, Zarqawi brought fighters, money and a plan.

21
00:04:11,153 --> 00:04:24,231
American decisions in May 2003 to purge members of Saddam's party and dissolve the army left hundreds of thousands of angry, armed men without work. Many were Sunnis.

22
00:04:26,431 --> 00:04:42,367
Ten days after the UN attack, a car bomb outside the Imam Ali shrine in Najaf, one of Shia Islam's holiest sites, killed a leading Shia cleric and more than eighty other people. Zarqawi's group later claimed both attacks.

23
00:04:43,467 --> 00:04:56,651
In a letter seized by US forces in early 2004, he set out his strategy. Attack Iraq's Shia majority, provoke them into revenge, and drive frightened Sunnis to his side.

24
00:04:57,751 --> 00:05:07,095
His suicide bombers struck markets, mosques, funerals, and police recruits queuing for jobs. Most of the victims were ordinary Iraqis.

25
00:05:08,195 --> 00:05:21,699
He also seized foreign hostages and filmed their murders. In 2004 his group killed an American, Nicholas Berg, and a British engineer from Liverpool, Kenneth Bigley, among others.

26
00:05:22,799 --> 00:05:37,433
His fighters helped make the city of Fallujah an insurgent stronghold. American and Iraqi forces stormed it in November 2004, in some of the heaviest street fighting of the war. Zarqawi escaped.

27
00:05:38,533 --> 00:05:46,064
That October he finally pledged allegiance to bin Laden, and his group became known as al-Qaeda in Iraq.

28
00:05:47,164 --> 00:05:55,356
He was now the most wanted man in Iraq. Yet he kept moving, mostly through the Sunni towns west and north of Baghdad.

29
00:05:57,556 --> 00:06:13,983
Even al-Qaeda's leaders worried. In 2005, US officials released a letter they said came from bin Laden's deputy, Ayman al-Zawahiri, urging him to stop attacking Shia mosques and filming killings. He did not stop.

30
00:06:15,083 --> 00:06:26,197
His old mentor, Maqdisi, also criticised him in public, arguing that bombings which killed Muslim civilians and attacks on ordinary Shia were wrong.

31
00:06:27,297 --> 00:06:39,201
In November 2005, his suicide bombers attacked three hotels in Amman, in his own country. More than fifty people were killed, many of them guests at a wedding.

32
00:06:40,301 --> 00:06:47,853
Jordanians were horrified. Thousands marched against him, and members of his own family publicly disowned him.

33
00:06:48,953 --> 00:07:01,369
In February 2006, bombers destroyed the golden dome of the Askari Shrine in Samarra, another sacred Shia site. The attack was blamed on al-Qaeda in Iraq.

34
00:07:02,469 --> 00:07:14,053
It unleashed what he had wanted. Shia militias and Sunni insurgents killed each other's communities, and over the following year tens of thousands of Iraqi civilians died.

35
00:07:16,253 --> 00:07:29,907
In April 2006 he showed his face on video for the first time, firing a machine gun in the desert. American forces later released unused footage of him fumbling with the weapon.

36
00:07:31,007 --> 00:07:43,828
US forces tracked one of his religious advisers to a house near Baqubah, north-east of Baghdad. On the seventh of June, 2006, an American jet dropped two bombs on it.

37
00:07:44,928 --> 00:07:51,243
Zarqawi was found in the rubble still alive, and died shortly afterwards. He was thirty-nine.

38
00:07:53,443 --> 00:08:04,216
His movement survived him. Months later, it renamed itself the Islamic State of Iraq. Under a later leader, Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi, it became ISIS.

39
00:08:05,316 --> 00:08:14,127
ISIS inherited his methods: war on the Shia, killings filmed as propaganda, and defiance even of al-Qaeda's own leaders.

40
00:08:15,227 --> 00:08:28,517
The monitoring group Iraq Body Count recorded about twelve thousand civilian deaths from violence in 2004, and around thirty thousand in 2006, the year he died.

41
00:08:29,617 --> 00:08:38,620
Most of the people killed by his movement were Iraqis: worshippers at prayer, shoppers, police recruits, and labourers waiting for work.

42
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:50,280
Zarqawi's goal was never only to kill. It was to turn neighbours against each other, so that the killing would go on without him. For years after his death, it did.
